ext":"கடல்மண்டு","lemma":"கடல்மண்டு","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"தோணியின்","parts":[{"text":"தோணியின்","lemma":"தோணியின்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"படைமுகம்","parts":[{"text":"படைமுகம்","lemma":"படைமுகம்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"போழ","parts":[{"text":"போழ","lemma":"போழ","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true}],"as_printed":true},{"words":[{"surface":"நெய்ம்மிதி","parts":[{"text":"நெய்ம்மிதி","lemma":"neymmiti","gloss":"mash mixed with ghee"}],"gloss":"mash mixed with ghee"},{"surface":"அருந்திய","parts":[{"text":"அருந்திய","lemma":"aruntiya","gloss":"consumed"}],"gloss":"consumed"},{"surface":"கொய்சுவல்","parts":[{"text":"கொய்சுவல்","lemma":"koycuval","gloss":"trimmed mane"}],"gloss":"trimmed mane"},{"surface":"எருத்தின்","parts":[{"text":"எருத்தின்","lemma":"eruttiṉ","gloss":"of the neck"}],"gloss":"of the neck"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"தண்ணடை","parts":[{"text":"தண்ணடை","lemma":"taṇṇaṭai","gloss":"fertile land"}],"gloss":"fertile land"},{"surface":"மன்னர்","parts":[{"text":"மன்னர்","lemma":"maṉṉar","gloss":"kings"}],"gloss":"kings"},{"surface":"தாருடை","parts":[{"text":"தாருடை","lemma":"tāruṭai","gloss":"wearing garlands"}],"gloss":"wearing garlands"},{"surface":"புரவி","parts":[{"text":"புரவி","lemma":"puravi","gloss":"horses"}],"gloss":"horses"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"அணங்கு","parts":[{"text":"அணங்கு","lemma":"aṇaṅku","gloss":"sacred power"}],"gloss":"sacred power"},{"surface":"உடை","parts":[{"text":"உடை","lemma":"uṭai","gloss":"possessing"}],"gloss":"possessing"},{"surface":"முருகன்","parts":[{"text":"முருகன்","lemma":"murukaṉ","gloss":"Murugan"}],"gloss":"Murugan"},{"surface":"கோட்டத்து","parts":[{"text":"கோட்டத்து","lemma":"kōṭṭattu","gloss":"of the temple"}],"gloss":"of the temple"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கலம்","parts":[{"text":"கலம்","lemma":"kalam","gloss":"vessel"}],"gloss":"vessel"},{"surface":"தொடா","parts":[{"text":"தொடா","lemma":"toṭā","gloss":"not touching"}],"gloss":"not touching"},{"surface":"மகளிரின்","parts":[{"text":"மகளிரின்","lemma":"makaḷiriṉ","gloss":"like women"}],"gloss":"like women"},{"surface":"இகழ்ந்து","parts":[{"text":"இகழ்ந்து","lemma":"ikaḻntu","gloss":"having shunned"}],"gloss":"having shunned"},{"surface":"நின்று","parts":[{"text":"நின்று","lemma":"niṉṟu","gloss":"having stood"}],"gloss":"having stood"},{"surface":"அவ்வே","parts":[{"text":"அவ்வே","lemma":"avvē","gloss":"they indeed"}],"gloss":"they indeed"}]}],"literal":"Cotton fence small village chief's / black gram chaff eaten tired gait horse, / like a sea-surging boat, battlefront to cleave, / ghee-mash consumed trimmed-mane necked / fertile land kings' garlanded horses, / sacred-power possessing Murugan temple's / vessel-untouching women-like, having shunned, stood indeed.","translation":"The horse of the chief of the small village with cotton fences, though of tired gait from eating only black gram chaff, cleaves through the battlefront like a boat surging through the sea. Seeing this, the garlanded horses of the kings of fertile lands stand back in shame, shunning the battlefront like menstruating women who stand outside, not touching any household vessels, at the temple of Murugan possessed of sacred power.","modern":"பருத்திச் செடிகளை வேலியாகக் கொண்ட சிறிய ஊரின் தலைவனுடைய குதிரை, உளுந்தின் உமியைத் தின்று தளர்ந்த நடையைக் கொண்டிருந்தாலும், கடலில் பாயும் தோணியைப் போலப் பகைவரின் படைமுகத்தைப் பிளந்து கொண்டு செல்கிறது. நெய் கலந்த உணவை உண்டு, கத்தரித்த பிடரி மயிரையும் வலிமையான கழுத்தையும் கொண்ட, மருத நிலத்து மன்னர்களின் மாலை அணிந்த குதிரைகளோ, தெய்வீக ஆற்றல் பொருந்திய முருகன் கோயிலில் கலங்களைத் தொடாமல் தள்ளி நிற்கும் பெண்களைப் போல, போர்க்களத்தில் ஈடுபடாமல் வெட்கி ஒதுங்கி நிற்கின்றன.","explanation":"The poet Pon Muḍiyār praises the valor of a small-town chief's horse in the Puram theme of Nochchi (defense of a fort) and Kuthirai Maram (the valor of the horse). The poet contrasts the humble horse of a small-town chief with the grand, well-fed horses of wealthy kings. Despite eating only dry black gram chaff and having a tired gait, the chief's horse bravely charges and cleaves through the enemy lines like a boat cutting through waves. In contrast, the pampered horses of the enemy kings, though fed on rich ghee-mash, stand back in shame and fear, refusing to enter the fight. The poet uses a striking simile, comparing the retreating, fearful horses to women during their menses who stand outside the sacred temple of Murugan, forbidden from touching any vessels. This vivid comparison highlights the absolute withdrawal and shame of the enemy forces when confronted by the raw courage of the small-town defender.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.6],[0.6,0.9],[0.9,1.0],[1.0,1.5],[1.5,1.9],[1.9,2.3],[2.7,3.0],[3.0,3.3],[3.3,4.3],[4.3,4.7],[4.7,5.3],[5.3,5.5],[5.6,6.7],[6.7,7.2],[7.2,7.7],[7.7,8.1],[8.1,9.0],[9.0,9.1],[9.1,9.54],[9.76,10.2],[10.2,10.9],[10.9,11.1],[11.1,11.3],[11.3,12.6],[12.6,13.25],[13.25,14.24],[14.36,14.6]],"female":[[0.0,0.6],[0.6,1.0],[1.0,1.1],[1.1,1.7],[1.7,2.35],[2.35,3.0],[3.0,3.7],[3.7,4.0],[4.0,5.1],[5.1,5.7],[5.7,6.36],[6.36,6.49],[6.49,6.97],[6.97,7.94],[7.94,8.17],[8.29,9.0],[9.0,9.18],[9.18,9.73],[9.73,10.28],[10.28,11.02],[11.2,12.0],[12.0,12.3],[12.3,12.6],[12.6,13.8],[13.8,14.7],[14.7,15.59],[15.59,15.73]]}}